  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nThe story in the long range forecast will be the battle between\ncool air masses pouring out of Canada beneath a trough in the \nEastern US and hot conditions building beneath the ridge over \nthe Southwest US. Our forecast area will frequently be at the\nboundary of these two air masses, with heat building to the west\nwhile cooler air hangs on to the east beneath northwesterly \nflow aloft. There will also be occasional chances for\nthunderstorms as impulses rotate around the ridge, but limited\nmoisture flowing north through the Plains will limit how\nfrequent or widespread these convective complexes may be. Latest\nNBM PoP remains quite low (20 percent or less) through the\nextended forecast, but Low Resolution Ensemble guidance does\nshow 30 to 50 percent probability of measurable rainfall in any\n24 hour period through this weekend, indicating greater\nconfidence that some storms will be around but timing and\nlocation uncertainty is keeping the PoP lower.\n\nConfidence is greatest in cooler conditions Thursday into\nFriday, with NBM high temperature interquartile range of 5 \ndegrees or less. However, uncertainty increases for the weekend \nwith that same interquartile range rising to 10 degrees or more\nSunday and Monday. If we stay more under the influence of the\ncooler air masses, temperatures will tend to top out in the 80s,\nbut if the hotter air from the ridge noses in, then we will see\ntemperatures approach 100 degrees again. Current NBM forecast is\na blend which does reflect a rather large gradient, hotter in \nthe west and cooler in the east.",
